{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"957a593367fb7bbe64ce377c49c2c7721a66173dd131e5814b782fe5fae223ec","slug":"skewt-parcel-path","location":"/weather_technology/skewt/skewt_parcel_path.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Skew-T Parcel Path","content_includes_title":false,"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","note":"All non-code content is licensed under CC-BY-4.0","free":true,"CC":true},"code":{"id":"Apache-2.0","url":"https://opensource.org/licenses/Apache-2.0","name":"Apache License 2.0","note":"All code content is licensed under Apache-2.0","free":true,"osi":true}},"github":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook","edit_url":null,"numbering":{"title":{"enabled":false,"offset":2},"equation":{"enabled":false},"figure":{"enabled":true,"continue":true}},"keywords":["metpy","python","meteorology","weather"],"copyright":"2026","settings":{"output_stderr":"remove-warn","output_matplotlib_strings":"remove"},"source_url":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook/blob/main/weather_technology/skewt/skewt_parcel_path.md","thumbnail":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook//build/finding_LCL-575724bf352b67f2466659a4f72910d6.jpg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"skewt_parcel_path.md","url":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook//build/skewt_parcel_path-258fe9b05a9a281d25634bdc965ed466.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skew-T diagrams are filled with lots of information; this part will\nintroduce you to a few concepts related to stability that can be\nanalyzed from an atmospheric sounding plotted on a skew-T diagram.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"nwtJT0f0jD"}],"key":"ivFq1zMMxM"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Drawing a Parcel Path","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"jnOs6iJhoo"}],"identifier":"drawing-a-parcel-path","label":"Drawing a Parcel Path","html_id":"drawing-a-parcel-path","implicit":true,"key":"vZPC0lHKbL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To draw a parcel path, you must first determine the lifting condensation\nlevel (LCL). In previous courses you did this through assuming a DALR\nand WALR, now we will use the skew-T/log-p diagram. The LCL is where\ncloud bases will form and thus the point of initial saturation in the\natmospheric column.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"Yh2UOqIM96"}],"key":"bA6VbOhiyO"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifting Condensation Level (LCL)","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"ioASzgtWY9"}],"identifier":"lifting-condensation-level-lcl","label":"Lifting Condensation Level (LCL)","html_id":"lifting-condensation-level-lcl","implicit":true,"key":"SfqIH4O7NY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition: The height at which a parcel of air lifted dry\nadiabatically would become saturated. The LCL is always found at or\nbelow the CCL.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"t7aok05Nmv"}],"key":"ov32FyknUN"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Procedure: Follow the saturation mixing ratio line from the surface\ndew-point and the dry adiabat from the surface temperature upward\nuntil they intersect.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"xko4xh32HU"}],"key":"HFT67rcy01"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook//build/finding_LCL-575724bf352b67f2466659a4f72910d6.jpg","alt":"finding the LCL","width":"750px","align":"center","key":"lVqGUzMjYq","urlSource":"../../images/finding_LCL.jpg"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"findingLCL","identifier":"findinglcl","html_id":"findinglcl","enumerator":"37","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"BIgLXb5EFV"},{"type":"text","value":"37","key":"cN5BIzkDK3"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"IkFNSf0tDO"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"c9UY87MXim"},{"type":"text","value":"This is a diagram to illustrate the finding of the Lifiting Condensation\nLevel (LCL).","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"JbTJfbiu95"}],"key":"lwWvP9mBhk"}],"key":"MpMRgWoPW8"},{"type":"legend","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source: ","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"mQtPqXJpSQ"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Use of The Skew T, Log P Diagram in Analysis and Forecasting","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"bCg0BseUoz"}],"key":"juEHxshSdx"},{"type":"text","value":"\nAWS/TR-79/006","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"vWdiTGlsWj"}],"key":"FiefKVuzPz"}],"key":"LzWEkTcLdI"}],"label":"findingLCL","identifier":"findinglcl","enumerator":"37","html_id":"findinglcl","key":"U3GT3j2326"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once the LCL is determined, then the parcel path can be drawn. Starting\nthe surface parcel at the temperature of the surface and following the\ndry adiabat line up until you reach the LCL, then lift the parcel via\nthe moist adiabatic line. (If you are not directly on a dry or moist\nadiabat, then follow parallel to the lines on either side of the parcel\npath.)","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"ZuhX9ZCXcb"}],"key":"mYHj94Seok"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook//build/drawing_parcel_path-752ce39c86e90eb0db9e2e8a3069a094.jpg","alt":"drawing the parcel path with the LCL","width":"750px","align":"center","key":"n8d0oLhkQF","urlSource":"../../images/drawing_parcel_path.jpg"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"parcelpath","identifier":"parcelpath","html_id":"parcelpath","enumerator":"38","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"dBp4UfzHUh"},{"type":"text","value":"38","key":"PggX19WnK8"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"RMsBofKTCD"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"MHmNsemDd9"},{"type":"text","value":"This is a diagram to illustrate of plotting the parcel path using the\nlifting condentation level (LCL).","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"XNsH5RnLhv"}],"key":"krUu8rkKJI"}],"key":"MrrGi2DWpC"},{"type":"legend","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source: ","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"YnTlHZL79P"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Use of The Skew T, Log P Diagram in Analysis and Forecasting","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"n0soXwe52W"}],"key":"kZCyyw51X4"},{"type":"text","value":"\nAWS/TR-79/006","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"Es6sKnpHLK"}],"key":"smXLHCwcrH"}],"key":"ld3htKJ0zS"}],"label":"parcelpath","identifier":"parcelpath","enumerator":"38","html_id":"parcelpath","key":"gYChRQ6dU5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As the result of drawing in the parcel path two other important levels\nare identified. The Level of Free Convection (LFC ) and the Equilibrium\nLevel (EL).","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"IDqAzmOp91"}],"key":"zuw0BnvgIE"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Level of Free Convection (LFC)","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"CZWHgVSiWO"}],"identifier":"level-of-free-convection-lfc","label":"Level of Free Convection (LFC)","html_id":"level-of-free-convection-lfc","implicit":true,"key":"zirYVwcBbi"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":66,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition: The level at which a parcel of air lifted dry\nadiabatically until saturated (LCL), then moist adiabatically becomes\n","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"key":"lUDIG5M55A"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"warmer","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"key":"lx9IE9owuB"}],"key":"zRJRcYTkfK"},{"type":"text","value":" than its environment.","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"key":"C0Ge5Tg8pM"}],"key":"w4Benzdp49"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":69,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Procedure: Find the LCL and then follow the moist adiabat upward until\nit intersects the temperature sounding. This is the LFC.","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"l1VXl71rdu"}],"key":"i4Em44eQ6R"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"K1lJ8f0mNo"}],"key":"TKCkRnJdNI"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Be careful when the LFC is below a sharp inversion. Ideally, one\nshould continue to follow the moist adiabat through the inversion to\ndetermine the LFC.","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"key":"O8TtM52wmT"}],"key":"bDRYLOOE6B"}],"key":"cKw2EEWGoI"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equilibrium Level (EL)","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"key":"zd24M6N9Fy"}],"identifier":"equilibrium-level-el","label":"Equilibrium Level (EL)","html_id":"equilibrium-level-el","implicit":true,"key":"yHcFau3332"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition: The level at which a parcel of air lifted moist\nadiabatically from the LFC becomes ","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"key":"Z0uBi8zbUf"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"cooler","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"key":"zuoDherU2J"}],"key":"KObsyYhBak"},{"type":"text","value":" than its environment. This\nis an estimate of the cloud height of convective type clouds.","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"key":"Xer5uHPHMA"}],"key":"omflcZwPdO"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Procedure: Find the LFC, then follow the moist adiabat upward until it\nintersects the temperature sounding. This is the EL.","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"key":"RRxKucxi28"}],"key":"pjQOGHDNQ2"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":92,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the parcel path is to the right of the environmental temperature\nprofile, the parcel is unstable and would want to rise. When it is to\nthe left of the environmental temperature profile the parcel is stable\nand would want to sink. If the parcel is stable in the lowest layers\n(i.e., the level below the LFC) then the atmosphere must force parcels\nto rise in order for the parcel to become unstable and realize any\ninstability that might be present in the Earth-atmosphere system.","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"key":"jLserohaEI"}],"key":"XruJmjlJDY"}],"key":"zxI74wTWgy"}],"key":"nlhh8U9Jte"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Practice Tasks - Basic MetPy SkewTs","url":"/metpy-skewt-practice","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"},"next":{"title":"Stability Indices","url":"/skewt-stability-indices","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}